  "worked_solution": "Let $G$ be the event that a green marble is selected, and let $A$ be the event that bag $A$ is selected. Then ${A}^{\u2032}$ is the event that bag $B$ is selected. \n\nTo calculate the probability that a selected marble is green, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(G)=P(G|A)P(A)+P(G|{A}^{\u2032})P({A}^{\u2032})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since there are $2$ bags, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=\\frac{1}{2},\\,P({A}^{\u2032})=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n- Since there are $3$ green marbles of the total of $10$ marbles in bag $A,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(G|A)=\\frac{3}{10}.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected marble in both bags is green is $\\frac{1}{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nP(G)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(G|{A}^{\u2032}),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(G) & =P(G|A)P(A)+P(G|{A}^{\u2032})P({A}^{\u2032}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =(\\frac{3}{10})(\\frac{1}{2})+P(G|{A}^{\u2032})(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{3}{20}+\\frac{1}{2}P(G|{A}^{\u2032}) \\\\ 20\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}) & =20\\cdot (\\frac{3}{20}+\\frac{1}{2}P(G|{A}^{\u2032}))\u200b \\\\ 10 & =3+10P(G|{A}^{\u2032}) \\\\ 7 & =10P(G|{A}^{\u2032}) \\\\ P(G|{A}^{\u2032}) & =\\frac{7}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected marble from bag $B$ is green is $\\frac{7}{10}.$",
